@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@ Phase 13. You write one scope file. The swarm splits it into small tickets, you
 2. `swarm scope check` flags missing acceptance, duplicate ids and vague words. It also lists the outside services that need an owner on day 0 (Stripe, domain, email, app stores).
 3. `swarm scope split` writes `docs/scope/split/manifest.json`: one job per stage. Run it with `swarm run docs/scope/split/manifest.json`.
 4. `swarm scope split --ingest RUN` reads the ticket lists from that run, assigns ids, adds the day 0 human tickets, runs the integrity checks and writes the ledger (`.swarm/project/tickets.json`), not approved. A bad file refuses the whole ingest and names the problem.
-5. `swarm scope approve` prints a short summary (stages, waves, estimate, risks). `swarm scope approve --yes` records your approval and writes `docs/scope/build-plan.md`. It refuses when coverage is below 100%, a ticket has no requirement, two parallel tickets write the same file, or the scope file changed after the split.
+5. `swarm scope approve` prints a short summary (stages, waves, estimate, risks). `swarm scope approve --yes` records your approval and writes `docs/scope/build-plan.md`. It refuses when coverage is below 100%, a ticket has no requirement, two parallel tickets write the same file, or the scope file changed after the split. **Ledger upgrade (1.167.29):** `ticketSetHash` no longer counts superseded tickets or proposed tickets that were never approved into the set, so the hash matches the live tickets only. A ledger approved under 1.167.28 that still lists superseded tickets reads as not approved after you upgrade; run `swarm scope approve --yes` once with no ticket changes to seal it again.
 6. `swarm go --scope [--max-jobs N] [--max-waves N]` builds the project, wave after wave, until the tickets are done or something needs you. Each wave does what used to be done by hand: it builds a manifest from the ready tickets (dependencies done), marks them running, runs it, integrates and commits it on the current branch (`Scope wave N`, or your `--commit-message`), then `swarm scope sync RUN` and `swarm scope sync RUN --integrated`, and it runs the security and acceptance reviews that step asks for (up to three rounds per run) before the next wave. It refuses an unapproved ledger, a ticket set edited since approval, or a scope file that is missing or changed since approval. Only one wave starts at a time (a lock). `--max-jobs` must be a positive whole number; `--max-waves` (1 to 500, default 50) bounds the loop and a second run continues where it stopped (exit 75). `--max-usd` and `--max-tokens` (or the profile's budget, else the `swarm go` default) cap each wave's run and each review run; with the project cap and `--max-waves` they bound the spend: at most waves times one wave plus its reviews, and no new run once the project total reaches the cap.
    - **Safety:** it refuses to start on `main`, `master` or a detached HEAD (`--allow-main-branch` overrides), and only one loop runs per project (never beside autopilot). The default branch is refused too, and the branch is checked again before each commit. Flags that make no sense for waves (`--concurrency`, `--leads`, `--batch`, `--timeout`, `--merge-method`, `--tag-timeout`, `--require-section`, `--exempt`) are refused. A wave commits only its own files and refuses when something else is staged. Ctrl-C or `swarm stop --all` ends it: the cancelled run's tickets stay running and nothing is synced, salvaged or reviewed after it.
    - **It stops, and says why, when:** every ticket is done (`complete`, exit 0); only human tickets are left (`waiting-human`, exit 0: `swarm tickets T-9001 --set done`); nothing is ready but tickets wait in review or are blocked (`stuck`); the project cap or a spend cap refuses the next run (`cap`; the wave's tickets go back to ready); `swarm stop --all` ran (`stopped`); a run is paused by a quota (`paused`, exit 75: `swarm resume RUN`; its tickets stay running); a wave's integrate fails its checks (`failed`: the next wave would build on unmerged code); four waves in a row finish no ticket (`no-progress`); or `--max-waves` is reached.
